settings-appearance-panel-fonts-blurb = Two faces: the one the interface reads in, and the one code is set in — the editor, the terminal and every code span. The bundled faces are open fonts shipped inside the app, so they look the same on every machine; <strong>System</strong> is whatever your OS already draws with and the fastest to paint.
settings-appearance-panel-theme-blurb = Five families, each with a light and a dark side: <strong>Glass</strong> frosts its panes over the desktop, <strong>Suede</strong> is matte, and the others are solid. <strong>System</strong> follows the OS — Glass, on whichever side the machine is set to.
# `<params/>` and `<account/>` are the two template forms, drawn as code by the caller.
settings-connectors-panel-shape-blurb = The rules the node holds it to: an https base URL (http on loopback only) whose host is in <code>hosts</code>, each host <code>host[:port]</code> or <code>*.suffix</code>; an auth scheme; operations with unique ids whose paths, queries and bodies read only <params/> declared on that operation and <account/> declared on the connector; no <code>Authorization</code>, <code>Host</code>, <code>Cookie</code>, <code>Content-Length</code> or <code>Transfer-Encoding</code> header of its own; a <code>file</code> parameter carried by a multipart part or a raw body, never a template; a <code>check</code> operation with no required parameter that writes nothing; an operation may say its own <code>timeout_secs</code>, a write its <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header, a read that selects a list how it <code>page</code>s; 64 KiB at most. Nothing here holds a credential. The guide's <em>Custom connector</em> section has the whole shape.
settings-git-profiles-panel-account-slot = account <account/>
settings-git-profiles-panel-key-slot = key <key/>
# `<dir/>` is the identity folder under the data directory.
settings-identity-panel-secret-key-held = Held in your OS keyring under the service <code>bisa</code>, or in <dir/> when the node runs with <code>--file-keys</code>.
settings-logging-panel-crashes-folder = <code>crashes/</code> — one report per abnormal end
settings-logging-panel-family-whose = <process/> — { $whose }
settings-mobile-development-panel-components-blurb = Each component, with what was found and the official way to install what was not. A change is read on the next look; <em>Check again</em> examines the machine now.
settings-pet-panel-blurb = A companion that floats over the app and shows what your agents are doing — running, waiting on you, or done. Drag it anywhere; click it to go to whatever it is reacting to. Nine ship with the platform; a pack of your own in Codex's format — a folder holding <code>pet.json</code> and a <code>spritesheet.webp</code> — imports below.
settings-security-panels-classifier-blurb = The classifier's session has no tools and no door to the platform; it reads the redacted call and answers one line. The rules decide what reaches it — a <code>classify</code> action on the Guard panel.
settings-security-panels-dash-reason = — { $reason }
